┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  1. INTRODUCTION                                                 │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Camelot is a two-player abstract strategy game combining the 
freedom of movement of medieval combat with elements of chess 
and checkers. Pieces begin in open battle formation near the 
center of the board rather than on the back ranks. The object 
is either to occupy the opponent’s two goal squares (the 
“castle”) with two of your own pieces, or to capture all of 
the opponent’s pieces while retaining at least two of your own.

The game emphasizes tactical combinations, multi-directional 
leaps, and the powerful “Knight’s Charge.”

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  2. EQUIPMENT                                                    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

• Board of 160 squares (checkered, alternating colors).
• Two sets of 14 pieces each:
    – 10 Men (foot soldiers / lower order)
    – 4 Knights (mounted / higher order)
• Pieces of contrasting colors (traditionally White/Red vs 
  Black/Yellow). Knights are distinguished by a head, 
  projection, or different shape/color marking.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  3. THE BOARD                                                    │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

The board is roughly rectangular but stepped at the ends:

  • Central body: 10 transverse rows of 12 squares.
  • Each end narrows symmetrically:
      – One row of 10 squares
      – One row of 8 squares
      – Two adjacent goal squares (the Castle) projecting 
        outward at mid-width.

The two Castle squares at each end are usually marked with 
stars or similar symbols. All squares are playable resting 
places for any piece. Movement is possible in eight directions 
(orthogonal and diagonal) on the existing squares.

White’s Castle occupies the two squares at the bottom end 
(commonly labeled F1 and G1).  
Black’s Castle occupies the two squares at the top end 
(commonly labeled F16 and G16).

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  4. SETUP                                                        │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Pieces start in opposing battle lines near the center of the 
board, with two empty transverse rows between the forces. 
Each side is well advanced from its own Castle.

Standard starting positions (files A–L left to right, ranks 
1–16 bottom to top; White at bottom):

  White Knights : C6, D7, I7, J6
  White Men     : D6, E6, E7, F6, F7, G6, G7, H6, H7, I6

  Black Knights : C11, D10, I10, J11
  Black Men     : D11, E10, E11, F10, F11, G10, G11, H10, H11, I11

(The board is often marked with dots or symbols indicating 
these starting squares.)

White moves first. Players alternate turns. On each turn a 
player must move exactly one of their own pieces.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  5. MOVEMENT RULES                                               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

All movement is in any of the eight directions (horizontal, 
vertical, or diagonal) along straight lines of adjacent 
squares. A piece may never land on or jump over an occupied 
square except as specifically allowed by the leap rules below.

There are four types of moves:

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.1  PLAIN MOVE  (Men and Knights)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Move one square in any direction to an adjacent empty square.
(Identical to the move of a King in chess.)

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.2  CANTER  (Men and Knights)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Leap over an adjacent friendly piece (Man or Knight) to the 
empty square immediately beyond it in a straight line.

• Multiple successive canters are allowed in a single turn.
• Direction may change after each individual leap.
• Friendly pieces leaped over remain on the board.
• A canter is always optional (never forced).
• A piece may not end a canter on the square from which it 
  began the move (no null moves / “passing”).

Purpose: rapid repositioning, advance, retreat, or support.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.3  JUMP (Capture)  (Men and Knights)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Leap over an adjacent enemy piece to the empty square 
immediately beyond it in a straight line, capturing and 
removing the enemy piece from the board.

• Multiple successive jumps are allowed (and required when 
  possible) in a single turn.
• Direction may change after each individual jump.
• Captured pieces are removed immediately.
• A series of jumps must continue until no further jump is 
  available from the landing square.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
5.4  KNIGHT’S CHARGE  (Knights only)
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
A single compound move consisting of one or more Canters 
followed immediately by one or more Jumps, in that order only.

• Canters first, then jumps. The two phases may not be mixed 
  or reversed.
• Multiple canters and multiple jumps are permitted.
• Direction may change after each individual leap.
• If, after any canter, the Knight lands next to an exposed 
  enemy piece that can be jumped, it must jump (unless a 
  different continuation of the same charge later captures 
  elsewhere).
• Once jumping begins, the Knight must continue jumping as 
  long as further captures are available.
• A Knight’s Charge is never obligatory in itself; however, 
  once a canter portion brings the Knight next to a capturable 
  enemy, the jump becomes required as part of that charge.

Men cannot perform a Knight’s Charge.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  6. CAPTURE OBLIGATIONS                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

• If any jump (ordinary or as part of a Knight’s Charge) is 
  available, the player must make a capturing move that turn.
• The obligation is satisfied by any legal capture (ordinary 
  Jump or Knight’s Charge). The player is not required to make 
  the longest possible capture.
• A pure canter or plain move is illegal when a capture is 
  possible.
• Exception: A Knight’s Charge is not forced merely because it 
  is possible. If the only way to capture is via a Knight’s 
  Charge, the player is not obliged to capture.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  7. CASTLE / GOAL RULES                                          │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

• The object squares (Castles) at each end are the two adjacent 
  goal squares.

• A player may not enter their own Castle by a Plain Move or 
  a Canter. A piece may land on its own Castle only by jumping 
  an enemy piece that stands adjacent to the Castle (or during 
  the jumping portion of a Knight’s Charge).

• If a piece jumps into its own Castle and can continue jumping 
  out, it must do so as part of the same move. If it cannot 
  continue, it must leave the Castle on the player’s next turn 
  (and must capture if a jump is available from that position).

• Once a piece enters the opponent’s Castle it may not leave 
  the Castle area (except to move between the two Castle 
  squares themselves). A player is limited to a small number 
  of such inter-Castle moves (commonly two per game under 
  modern interpretations).

• A player may not permanently occupy their own Castle merely 
  to block the opponent.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  8. WINNING CONDITIONS                                           │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

A player wins by achieving any one of the following:

  1. Occupying both of the opponent’s Castle squares with any 
     two of their own pieces (Men or Knights).

  2. Capturing all of the opponent’s pieces while still 
     retaining two or more of their own pieces.

  3. Having two or more pieces remaining while the opponent 
     has no legal move.

The game is a draw if both players are reduced to one piece 
(or fewer) each.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│  9. ADDITIONAL NOTES                                             │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

• Pieces of either type may occupy any square on the board 
  (no restricted zones except the special Castle entry rules).

• There is no “flying” or long-range non-leaping movement; all 
  leaps are short (over one adjacent piece to the square 
  immediately beyond).

• The game has no element of chance. Skill, calculation of 
  multi-leap combinations, and control of the center and 
  flanks decide the outcome.

• The original patent stresses the simulation of open medieval 
  battlefield combat: forces start advanced, pieces may freely 
  advance, retire, or move laterally/diagonally, and Knights 
  can charge through friendly ranks into the enemy.

┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ 10. EXAMPLE SEQUENCE (from the patent description)               │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

A typical long Knight’s Charge might involve:

  Canter over a friendly Knight → land  
  Canter laterally over a friendly Man → land  
  Jump diagonally over an enemy Man (capture) → land  
  Jump forward over another enemy Man (capture) → land  
  Jump diagonally backward over an enemy Knight (capture) → land  
  Jump over a final enemy Knight (capture) → final landing square.

Such multi-directional combinations are the hallmark of high-level 
play.

